WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.293
- [Voiceover] We already know a little bit

00:01.293 --> 00:02.467
about square roots.

00:02.467 --> 00:03.800
For example, if I were to tell you

00:03.800 --> 00:07.388
that seven squared is equal to 49,

00:07.388 --> 00:09.864
that's equivalent to saying that seven

00:09.864 --> 00:13.668
is equal to the square root of 49.

00:13.668 --> 00:16.269
The square root essentially unwinds

00:16.269 --> 00:18.110
taking the square of something.

00:18.110 --> 00:19.565
In fact, we could write it like this.

00:19.565 --> 00:22.651
We could write the square root of 49,

00:22.651 --> 00:24.709
so this is whatever number times itself

00:24.709 --> 00:26.366
is equal to 49.

00:26.366 --> 00:29.027
If I multiply that number times itself,

00:29.027 --> 00:32.281
if I square it, well I'm going to get 49.

00:32.281 --> 00:34.422
And that's going to be
true for any number,

00:34.942 --> 00:36.974
not just 49.

00:38.334 --> 00:41.683
If I write the square root of X

00:42.183 --> 00:43.981
and if I were to square it,

00:43.981 --> 00:46.429
that's going to be equal to X

00:46.429 --> 00:48.781
and that's going to be true for any X

00:48.781 --> 00:52.002
for which we can evaluate the square root,

00:52.002 --> 00:54.080
evaluate the principle root.

00:54.080 --> 00:57.236
Now typically and as you advance in math

00:57.236 --> 01:00.236
you're going to see that this will change,

01:00.236 --> 01:01.985
but typically you say,
okay if I'm going to take

01:01.985 --> 01:05.805
the square root of something,
X has to be non-negative.

01:05.805 --> 01:09.395
X has to be non-negative.

01:09.945 --> 01:13.972
This is going to change
once we start thinking about

01:13.972 --> 01:15.901
imaginary and complex numbers,

01:15.901 --> 01:18.671
but typically for the
principle square root,

01:18.671 --> 01:21.464
we assume that whatever's
under the radical,

01:21.464 --> 01:25.509
whatever's under here, is
going to be non-negative

01:25.509 --> 01:26.902
because it's hard to square a number

01:26.902 --> 01:28.740
at least the numbers that we know about,

01:28.740 --> 01:31.580
it's hard to square them
and get a negative number.

01:31.580 --> 01:34.974
So for this thing to be
defined, for it to make sense,

01:34.974 --> 01:37.106
it's typical to say
that, okay we need to put

01:37.106 --> 01:39.184
a non-negative number in here.

01:39.184 --> 01:41.225
But anyway, the focus of this video

01:41.225 --> 01:44.109
is not on the square root, it's
really just to review things

01:44.109 --> 01:47.863
so we can start thinking
about the cube root.

01:47.863 --> 01:50.201
And as you can imagine,
where does the whole notion

01:50.201 --> 01:53.768
of taking a square of something
or a square root come from?

01:53.768 --> 01:55.147
Well it comes from the notion of finding

01:55.147 --> 01:56.784
the area of a square.

01:56.784 --> 02:00.580
If I have a square like this
and if this side is seven,

02:00.580 --> 02:03.264
well if it's a square, all the
sides are going to be seven.

02:03.264 --> 02:05.687
And if I wanted to find the area of this,

02:05.687 --> 02:09.078
it would be seven times
seven or seven squared.

02:09.078 --> 02:11.944
That would be the area of this.

02:11.944 --> 02:14.421
Or if I were to say, well what is

02:14.421 --> 02:17.166
if I have a square, if I have,

02:17.166 --> 02:18.956
and that doesn't look
like a perfect square,

02:18.956 --> 02:21.608
but you get the idea, all the
sides are the same length.

02:21.608 --> 02:25.039
If I have a square with area X.

02:25.039 --> 02:27.037
If the area here is X,

02:27.037 --> 02:29.232
what are the lengths of
the sides going to be?

02:29.232 --> 02:31.425
Well it's going to be square root of X.

02:31.425 --> 02:33.260
All of the sides are
going to be the square root of X,

02:33.260 --> 02:35.964
so it's going to be the square root
of X by the square root of X

02:35.964 --> 02:37.776
and this side is going to
be the square root of X as well

02:37.776 --> 02:39.261
and that's going to be
the square root of X as well.

02:39.261 --> 02:41.943
So that's where the term
square root comes from,

02:41.943 --> 02:44.277
where the square comes from.

02:44.277 --> 02:46.363
Now what do you think cube root?

02:46.363 --> 02:47.864
Well same idea.

02:47.864 --> 02:49.775
If I have a cube.

02:50.205 --> 02:52.055
If I have a cube.

02:52.055 --> 02:55.317
Let me do my best attempt at
drawing a cube really fast.

02:55.317 --> 02:59.314
If I have a cube and a
cube, all of it's dimensions

02:59.314 --> 03:03.669
have the same length so this
is a two, by two, by two cube,

03:03.669 --> 03:05.876
what's the volume over here.

03:05.876 --> 03:10.348
Well the volume is going to
be two, times two, times two,

03:10.348 --> 03:15.232
which is two to the
third power or two cubed.

03:15.232 --> 03:17.014
This is two cubed.

03:17.014 --> 03:18.895
That's why they use the word cubed

03:18.895 --> 03:20.879
because this would be the volume of a cube

03:20.879 --> 03:23.379
where each of its sides have length two

03:23.379 --> 03:26.370
and this of course is
going to be equal to eight.

03:26.370 --> 03:28.233
But what if we went the other way around?

03:28.233 --> 03:30.740
What if we started with the cube?

03:30.740 --> 03:32.979
What if we started with this volume?

03:33.349 --> 03:35.809
What if we started with a cube's volume

03:35.809 --> 03:40.450
and let's say the volume
here is eight cubic units,

03:40.450 --> 03:43.930
so volume is equal to eight

03:43.930 --> 03:46.857
and we wanted to find
the lengths of the sides.

03:46.857 --> 03:51.180
So we wanted to figure out what X is

03:51.180 --> 03:53.606
cause that's X, that's X, and that's X.

03:53.606 --> 03:56.810
It's a cube so all the
dimensions have the same length.

03:56.810 --> 03:58.512
Well there's two ways that
we could express this.

03:58.512 --> 04:01.280
We could say that X times X times X

04:01.280 --> 04:05.158
or X to the third power is equal to eight

04:05.158 --> 04:08.640
or we could use the cube root symbol,

04:08.640 --> 04:11.837
which is a radical with a
little three in the right place.

04:12.677 --> 04:15.838
Or we could write that X is equal to,

04:15.838 --> 04:18.526
it's going to look very
similar to the square root.

04:18.526 --> 04:20.238
This would be the square root of eight,

04:20.238 --> 04:21.526
but to make it clear,
they were talking about

04:21.526 --> 04:23.914
the cube root of eight, we would write

04:23.914 --> 04:26.054
a little three over there.

04:26.054 --> 04:27.261
In theory for square root,

04:27.261 --> 04:28.782
you could put a little two over here,

04:28.782 --> 04:29.656
but that'd be redundant.

04:29.656 --> 04:30.825
If there's no number here,

04:30.825 --> 04:33.715
people just assume that
it's the square root.

04:33.715 --> 04:36.641
But if you're figuring out the cube root

04:36.641 --> 04:38.775
or sometimes you say the third root,

04:38.775 --> 04:39.743
well then you have to say,

04:39.743 --> 04:41.738
well you have to put this
little three right over here

04:41.738 --> 04:46.161
in this little notch in the
radical symbol right over here.

04:46.161 --> 04:48.354
And so this is saying X
is going to be some number

04:48.354 --> 04:51.617
that if I cube it, I get eight.

04:51.617 --> 04:52.476
So with that out of the way,

04:52.476 --> 04:54.544
let's do some examples.

04:54.544 --> 04:56.583
Let's say that I have...

04:56.583 --> 04:59.174
Let's say that I want to calculate

05:00.934 --> 05:03.284
the cube root of 27.

05:03.284 --> 05:04.752
What's that going to be?

05:04.752 --> 05:07.540
Well if say that this is
going to be equal to X,

05:07.540 --> 05:09.902
this is equivalent to
saying that X to the third

05:09.902 --> 05:14.355
or that 27 is equal to
X to the third power.

05:14.355 --> 05:16.491
So what is X going to be?

05:17.001 --> 05:19.653
Well X times X times X is equal to 27,

05:19.653 --> 05:23.154
well the number I can think of is three,

05:23.154 --> 05:26.850
so we would say that X, let
me scroll down a little bit,

05:26.850 --> 05:28.953
X is equal to three.

05:28.953 --> 05:31.156
Now let me ask you a question.

05:32.256 --> 05:34.413
Can we write something like...

05:34.413 --> 05:36.355
Can we pick a new color?

05:36.355 --> 05:41.355
The cube root of, let
me write negative 64.

05:41.993 --> 05:43.359
I already talked about
that if we're talking

05:43.359 --> 05:45.495
the square root, it's fairly typical that

05:45.495 --> 05:47.097
hey you put a negative number in there

05:47.097 --> 05:48.850
at least until we learn
about imaginary numbers,

05:48.850 --> 05:50.121
we don't know what to do with it.

05:50.121 --> 05:52.605
But can we do something with this?

05:53.085 --> 05:57.342
Well if I cube something,
can I get a negative number?

05:57.342 --> 05:58.444
Sure.

05:58.914 --> 06:01.173
So if I say this is equal to X,

06:01.173 --> 06:04.445
this is the same thing as
saying that negative 64

06:04.445 --> 06:07.006
is equal to X to the third power.

06:07.006 --> 06:09.097
Well what could X be?

06:09.097 --> 06:11.540
Well what happens if
you take negative four

06:11.540 --> 06:15.180
times negative four times negative four?

06:15.180 --> 06:17.523
Negative four times four is positive 16,

06:17.523 --> 06:21.297
but then times negative
four is negative 64

06:21.297 --> 06:23.423
is equal to negative 64.

06:23.423 --> 06:25.103
So what could X be here?

06:25.103 --> 06:27.722
Well X could be equal to negative four.

06:27.722 --> 06:31.112
X could be equal to negative four.

06:31.112 --> 06:32.708
So based on the math that we know so far

06:32.708 --> 06:37.708
you actually can take the cube
root of a negative number.

06:37.838 --> 06:39.885
And just so you know, you
don't have to stop there.

06:39.885 --> 06:40.904
You could take a fourth root

06:40.904 --> 06:42.471
and in this case you'd have a four here,

06:42.471 --> 06:43.469
a fifth root, a sixth root,

06:43.469 --> 06:45.385
a seventh root of numbers

06:45.385 --> 06:48.368
and we'll talk about that later

06:48.368 --> 06:49.761
in your mathematical career.

06:49.761 --> 06:50.771
But most of what you're going to see

06:50.771 --> 06:51.770
is actually going to be square root

06:51.770 --> 06:53.627
and every now and then you're
going to see a cube root.

06:53.627 --> 06:54.962
Now you might be saying, well hey look,

06:54.962 --> 06:58.511
you know, you just knew that
three to the third power is 27,

06:58.511 --> 06:59.768
you took the cube root, you get X,

06:59.768 --> 07:02.046
is there any simple way to do this?

07:02.046 --> 07:04.609
And like you know if i give
you an arbitrary number.

07:04.609 --> 07:07.000
If I were to just say, I don't know,

07:07.000 --> 07:12.000
if I were to say cube root of 125.

07:12.416 --> 07:13.989
And the simple answer is,

07:13.989 --> 07:17.542
well the easiest way to
actually figure this out

07:17.542 --> 07:20.312
is actually just to do a factorization

07:20.312 --> 07:22.998
and particular prime
factorization of this thing

07:22.998 --> 07:25.206
right over here and then
you would figure it out.

07:25.206 --> 07:29.369
So you would say, okay
well 125 is five times 25,

07:29.369 --> 07:31.624
which is five times five.

07:31.624 --> 07:36.024
Alright, so this is the same
thing as the cube root of five

07:36.024 --> 07:37.765
to the third power, which of course,

07:37.765 --> 07:41.979
is going to be equal to five.

07:41.979 --> 07:43.302
If you have a much larger number here,

07:43.302 --> 07:47.905
yes, there's no very simple way to compute

07:47.905 --> 07:49.798
what a cube root or a fourth root

07:49.798 --> 07:51.755
or a fifth root might
be and even square root

07:51.755 --> 07:53.960
can get quite difficult.

07:53.960 --> 07:56.305
There's no very simple
way to just calculate it

07:56.305 --> 07:59.248
the way that you might
multiply things or divide it.
